  "description": "Joint PR #638 + #639 operator-valued Φ_q probe under the per-partition k_λ rule, sweeping non-dominant depth N ∈ {1, 2, 3, 4, 5}. Tests whether pushing the non-dominant Wedderburn blocks to deeper truncation approaches PR #639's substrate c(K) ceiling (N=3 → 9.4 ppb, N=4 → 0.092 ppb). Implements TWO selectors for the dominant partition: (A) max-|μ| — the selector PR #639's `per-partition-k-lambda-rule.md` states; (B) max-|y·contrib_k=2| — the selector whose values match PR #639's table. Both are run for each N so the gap between the .md's stated rule, its table, and the binding-formula reality is on the record. Eigenvalues via numpy.linalg.eigvals on Hoefsmit seminormal ρ_λ(β); per-eigenvalue Φ_q via the global `phi_q_truncated_complex` (with pole guard); Wedderburn-summed with signed y_λ(q_0); |·| at binding-formula level. Note: this probe records `allPassed: false` — under both selectors the worst-case error at N=2 is ~50%, not the ~5% PR #639's .md claims; full reconciliation in `docs/audits/2026-05-11-per-partition-k-lambda-selector-investigation.md`. Cherry-picks (per /coordinate §10): #638's eigenvalue / operator-trace infrastructure + #639's per-partition k_λ rule and selector candidates + #639's c(K) ceiling for comparison.",
